- IP Addressing:
Now, we are going to cover another important topic: IP addresses. This is also important for discussion, as well as for our device security and the way communication between devices is established, which determines the range within which devices or nodes can send or receive data. 

- Definition: IP stands for Internet Protocol. Think of an IP address like the return address of your computer that decides how data is sent and how your device or the devices receive data. This is the logical address of your device or each node on the network that is able to communicate with other devices or nodes. 
When a device tries to connect to a network or device, an IP is assigned to this device so that it can communicate with other devices, and after this,  the device will be able to send and receive data. These IP addresses are assigned by an ISP (Internet Service Provider) when we connect to a WAN network, and when we connect to a local network, this task is handled by DHCP(Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol). 
There are 2 versions (IPv4 & IPv6) of IP address:
        1. IPv4: The Internet Protocol version four is an IP address that is written in 4 parts and separated by dots or periods like 198.165.21.3 or 0.0.0.0, something like this. It uses the capacity of 32 bits of numbers and almost 4.3 million combinations. This version uses a subnet mask that decides the range of this IP address. After some time, with the repeated increasing of IOT devices, the world feels a shortage of IP addresses. 
Then, we need another method of assigning IP addresses with a large number of IP addresses, which is IPv6.
        2. IPv6: An IP address written in hexadecimal numbers. This is the combination of letters and numbers separated by semicolons. e.g., 2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334.   It has an infinite number of combinations to meet human needs. This version has a 128-bit number capacity. 
There are two types of IP addresses for IoT devices on the networks:
        1. Public IP: When we connect directly to the Internet, the IP address assigned by the ISP is called our public IP address. The ISP has a larger number of IPs, and these are used to communicate and identify different devices on the Internet. When we connect to LAN networks, this address is provided to our router, and then this way, we will also be able to access global information even on a local area network.
        2. Private IP: The devices in a LAN network have an IP address that is managed by DHCP. For example, mobile phones, laptops, printers, etc, get an IP address from DHCP. This system works on this process: D O R A, where D means discover, O means offer, R means request, and A means acknowledge.
The system first discovers and checks for the availability of IP addresses within its ranges and then offers them to the devices with an acknowledgement action. This enhances the security and reliability of a network. This is normally best for an office, a university, or a small network. But again, to access the information or data from outside the network, we need a public IP address, and here we use gateways and routers which use to decide the best path and translate the traffic according to protocols for a foreign network to communicate with it outside our LAN networks.

There are two types of IP addresses in public IPs.
        1. Static IP: An IP address that does not change when we connect again and again to a WAN network. These IPs are normally for servers when we want to host our website personally. This is a permanent IP when it is assigned.
        2. Dynamic IP: Our ISP has a large number of IPs. When a device connects to the Internet, an IP is assigned by the ISP, and when the device is disconnected, this IP will be reserved for another device. This IP provides us with another benefit, too, by providing an extra layer of security, which means when our IP changes frequently, it is very hard for an attacker when he is trying to track our online activities.

There are two types of websites' IP addresses:
       1. Shared IP: Websites that run on shared hosting plans and get a shared IP address. This means a server that runs multiple websites simultaneously; the websites hosted on this server share a single IP address. This technique is cost-saving and also beneficial for security purposes. But the traffic of a site and the number of pages are limited and manageable.
       2. Dedicated IP: Websites that are hosted on a personal hosting server get a dedicated IP address. This is a static IP address and never changes once it is allocated. After getting a dedicated IP, a website is also able to run its own FTP for communication.

- Subnet Mask and CIDR notation.
These are two techniques for doing the same job.
       1. Subnet mask:  The subnet mask decides the range and size of a network. It divides an IP address into two parts. The first three octets, e.g., 198.2.21, decide the network address, and the last part tells us the host number. A private IP address also has a subnet mask, which ranges from 0.0.0.0 to 255.255.255.255.
This is the hidden part of an IP address. Need for a submask because our systems only understand data in 0s and 1s form.  For example, the IP address is 198.168.1.50, and the subnet mask is 255.255.255.0. When the request goes on the network, this mask is translated into 0s and 1s form; for a subnet mask 255.255.255.0, the binary form is 11111111.11111111.11111111. 00000000. To know about the range of a network, we count the 1s in binary data, which tells us how, actually, the size of our network. Like in previous binary numbers, we have 24 1s, which tells us this is a small network, like for a home or for a small corporate office. 
If we look at the previous IP address, 198.168.1 is the network ID, and the next number, like 50, is the host ID in the network
2. CIDR notation: CIDR stands for Classless Inter-Domain Routing. We need this CIDR notation because writing a subnet mask after an IP address is not a good practice. This method converts a whole subnet mask in binary to a single or double-digit form. Like in previous examples, for a subnet mask 255.255.255.0, when we convert it into binary form, this becomes 11111111.11111111.11111111. 00000000. So we see this is too long for practical writing. So, the CIDR notation gives us a method to count only 1s in a binary string. 
As if we count the 1s in the previous binary form subnet mask, which is 24, we write this number with an IP address that tells us the IP of a host as well as its range or size. Like the IP address 198.168.1.50/24, the last digits 24 are the size of a network. 
The common subnet mask in CIDR form:
      /24 (24 ones) for a small network at the office or home and offers 256 IPs.
      /8 (8 ones) very large networks that can accept more than 16 million host devices
      /16 (16 ones) is a medium network that accepts almost 65000 devices
      /32 (32 ones) that a small connection between two devices.
